// =============================================================================
// package.lumen — the Package pack, authored as Lumen (the kind model)
// =============================================================================
// Defines the PACKAGES mechanism: how op/channel/type DECLARATIONS reflect to
// beads, how those beads form a Package (a rooted bead graph), how the package
// format IS the BDP export format (linearize from the root), and the pack-
// management surface (install / uninstall / list / show / lock).
//
// There is no "core" layer: pack-management is ITSELF a pack, and even `core` is
// a pack — declarations only, identity pinned to the BDP spec, version 0.2.0.
//
// Depends on core (bdp.lumen): bead, resource, ref, unpinned, pinned, Operation,
// Channel, BeadType, ExportRecord/import, source, Problem, Outcome, selector,
// ListFilter, LocalName. Example packs in the kind model: memory.lumen.
// =============================================================================

/// description: A semantic version string, "MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH" (e.g. "0.2.0").
schema Semver = string
/// description: A version-constraint expression (e.g. "^0.2", ">=0.2 <0.3"). Matching semantics — see §7.
schema SemverConstraint = string


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1. Definition — a declaration, reflected as a bead
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Definition — one type / operation / channel DECLARATION, reflected as a bead. SEALED
/// normative: into the three declaration kinds: a TYPE declaration → TypeDecl (a BeadType bead); an
/// normative: OPERATION → OpDecl (an Operation bead); a CHANNEL → ChannelDecl (a Channel bead). This is
/// normative: the "declarations translate to beads" rule. Their TYPE SIGNATURES carry the type-descriptor
/// normative: AST (TypeDef) on the Lumen SURFACE and lower to `schema`/JsonSchema on the wire — the same
/// normative: descriptor applies to type, operation AND channel declarations uniformly.
kind Definition is bead = TypeDecl | OpDecl | ChannelDecl


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2. Package — a rooted bead graph
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Package — a rooted graph of the Definition beads a pack contributes. The package FORMAT
/// normative: is the BDP export format: `export` rooted here, linearized to ExportRecords (§5). `dependsOn`
/// normative: names other packs by constraint; `pin` (on each Dependency) is written by `lock`, not here.
kind Package is bead {
  name:         string { minLength: 1 },
  version:      Semver,
  declarations: set<ref<Definition>>,      // the beads this pack contributes (the graph)
  dependsOn?:   set<Dependency>,
}

/// description: Dependency — a reference to another pack. `constraint` stays LIVE and never mutates;
/// normative: `pin` is a PARALLEL field written by `lock` (resolve-then-pin, the constraint retained).
/// normative: `alias` is OPEN (#4) — import aliasing vs BDP scope-alias is unresolved.
schema Dependency = {
  package:    string,
  constraint: SemverConstraint,            // STAYS live, never mutated
  pin?:       ref<Package>,                // PARALLEL field, written by `lock`
  alias?:     LocalName,                   // OPEN #4
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §3. sys/packages — the installed registry
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Packages — a GENERIC container of package refs (reusable: an install set, a transitive
/// normative: closure, a lock resolution). "Installed" is POSITIONAL, not a field name: the service anoints
/// normative: ONE intrinsic `Packages` bead at the well-known id `sys/packages` as authoritative, and ITS
/// normative: `packages` are the installed set. Install/uninstall are visible on the change feed as ordinary
/// normative: mutations to it — no bespoke install event. A `list` over `packages` enumerates what is installed.
/// normative: Storage class is Intrinsic (service-declared).
type Packages is bead {
  packages: set<ref<Package>>,
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §4. The package format — the BDP export format
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// normative: A Package has NO bespoke serialization. Its exported form is `export` rooted at the Package
/// normative: bead, linearized to ExportRecords; `packInstall` is `import` of that stream plus anointing
/// normative: the root into sys/packages. This is the lumendoc graph↔export isomorphism: the pack format
/// normative: IS the export of the package graph, so linearize-the-root and install-is-import come for free.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §5. The pack-management surface (callable operations; CLI projection noted)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// These are CALLABLE operations the pack contributes (Operation beads, invoked via
// `call`) — NOT new core primitives. The CLI is their BDP projection (dogfood).

/// description: Install a pack — import its exported graph, then register the root in sys/packages.
/// bdp: callable; cli `pack install <exportedpack>`   // flags tbd
command packInstall(pack: source<ExportRecord>, strategy?: ImportStrategy) returns ref<Package> fails Problem

/// description: Uninstall a pack — drop its ref from sys/packages. OPEN (#2): the fate of Definition beads
/// normative: another installed pack still references (refcount vs leave-and-sweep).
/// bdp: callable; cli `pack uninstall <ref<Package>>`
command packUninstall(package: unpinned<Package>) returns Outcome<Package, Problem> fails Problem

/// description: List installed packs — `list` over sys/packages.packages, applying filter and projection.
/// bdp: callable; cli `pack list --filter --select`
command packList(where?: ListFilter, select?: selector) returns [Package] fails Problem

/// description: Show one pack — `get` on the root.
/// bdp: callable; cli `pack show <ref<Package>>`
command packShow(package: ref<Package>) returns Package fails Problem

/// description: Lock — resolve dependency constraints and write the parallel `pin` fields. Surface TBD;
/// normative: harvest the lock/pin ergonomics from gas city `bd pack` (NOT its construct-a-pack-via-CLI surface).
/// bdp: callable; cli `pack lock`   // relock flags tbd
command packLock(package: unpinned<Package>) returns pinned<Package> fails Problem


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §6. `core` as a pack
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// normative: `core` is itself a Package — declarations only, no implementations. Its identity is pinned to
/// normative: the BDP spec; its version is the spec semver (0.2.0). It is frozen.
/// normative: core = Package { name: "core", version: "0.2.0", declarations: <the six primitives + core schemas>, dependsOn: none }


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §7. OPEN — the frontier (pending the lead's rulings)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// > Open #1 (identity + resolution) — do Definition beads get scope-global or pack-scoped identity? This
// >   decides cross-pack refs, collisions, and whether two packs share a dependency bead. Underpins #2 and #4.
// > Open #2 (GC on uninstall) — on drop, the fate of Definition beads a surviving pack still references.
// > Open #4 (aliasing) — import alias (pack-level `as`) vs BDP scope-alias: one mechanism or two, and how they compose.
// > Open (TypeDef placement) — TypeDef as a Lumen-surface/reflection form that lowers to JsonSchema, vs core
// >   wire. Held pending ruling; decides whether op/channel signatures carry TypeDef or raw `schema`.
// > Open (operation implementations in the pack) — a pack may carry operation IMPLEMENTATIONS, not just
// >   declarations (the runtime owner is wiring this). To be jammed and specced: how an impl rides in the
// >   pack (bead? formula?), how it binds to its OpDecl, and how it reaches the scheduler.
// > Open (semver matching) — constraint-matching rules and the `lock` resolution algorithm.
